content:
To address the problem of creating a calculator app based on the given requirements, we will structure the solution into three main components: arithmetic, calculator, and main entry. Each component will be implemented in C, ensuring modularization and separation of concerns.

### Approach
1. **Arithmetic Component**: This component will handle the basic arithmetic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) and return the result. Special care will be taken to handle division by zero gracefully by returning NaN.

2. **Calculator Component**: This component will handle user interaction in interactive mode by displaying a menu and reading input values. It will also validate the input and ensure the correct format. In non-interactive mode, it will interpret command-line arguments and pass them to the arithmetic component.

3. **Main Entry Point**: This will determine the mode of operation (interactive or non-interactive) based on command-line arguments. It will integrate the two components and handle execution, including error checking and graceful termination.

### Solution Code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <limits>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define INTERACTIVE_MODE

double add(double a, double b) {
    return a + b;
}

double subtract(double a, double b) {
    return a - b;
}

double multiply(double a, double b) {
    return a * b;
}

double divide(double a, double b) {
    if (std::abs(b) < std::numeric_limits<double>::epsilon()) {
        return NAN;
    }
    return a / b;
}

void calculator_run_interactive() {
    int choice;
    do {
        printf("1. Addition\n");
        printf("2. Subtraction\n");
        printf("3. Multiplication\n");
        printf("4. Division\n");
        printf("5. Exit\n");
        scanf("%d", &choice);
    } while (choice < 1 || choice > 5);

    if (choice == 5) {
        return;
    }

    char* op = NULL;
    if (choice == 1) op = "+";
    else if (choice == 2) op = "-";
    else if (choice == 3) op = "*";
    else if (choice == 4) op = "/";

    if (op == NULL) {
        printf("Invalid operation\n");
        return;
    }

    double val1, val2;
    do {
        printf("Enter first value: ");
        if (scanf("%lf", &val1) != 1) {
            printf("Invalid number format. Please try again.\n");
            fflush(stdin);
        }
    } while (val1 == NAN || val1 == INFINITY);

    do {
        printf("Enter second value: ");
        if (scanf("%lf", &val2) != 1) {
            printf("Invalid number format. Please try again.\n");
            fflush(stdin);
        }
    } while (val2 == NAN || val2 == INFINITY);

    double result;
    if (strcmp(op, "+")) {
        result = add(val1, val2);
    } else if (strcmp(op, "-")) {
        result = subtract(val1, val2);
    } else if (strcmp(op, "*")) {
        result = multiply(val1, val2);
    } else if (strcmp(op, "/")) {
        result = divide(val1, val2);
    } else {
        printf("Invalid operation\n");
        return;
    }

    if (result == NAN) {
        printf("Division by zero\n");
        return;
    }

    printf("Result: %.10f\n", result);
}

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    if (argc == 0) {
        calculator_run_interactive();
    } else if (argc == 3) {
        char* op = argv[1];
        double val1 = strtod(argv[2]);
        double val2 = strtod(argv[3]);

        if (op == NULL || val1 == NAN || val2 == NAN) {
            printf("Usage: calculator <op> <val1> <val2>\n");
            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
        }

        if (strcmp(op, "+") != 0 && strcmp(op, "-") != 0 && strcmp(op, "*") != 0 && strcmp(op, "/") != 0) {
            printf("Invalid operation\n");
            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
        }

        double result;
        if (strcmp(op, "+")) {
            result = add(val1, val2);
        } else if (strcmp(op, "-")) {
            result = subtract(val1, val2);
        } else if (strcmp(op, "*")) {
            result = multiply(val1, val2);
        } else if (strcmp(op, "/")) {
            if (val2 == 0.0) {
                printf("Division by zero\n");
                exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
            }
            result = divide(val1, val2);
        }

        printf("Result: %.10f\n", result);
        exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
    } else {
        printf("Usage: calculator [interactive] or calculator op val1 val2\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
}
```

### Explanation
1. **Arithmetic Component**: The functions `add`, `subtract`, `multiply`, and `divide` handle the arithmetic operations. The `divide` function checks for division by zero using a threshold to determine if the denominator is effectively zero.

2. **Calculator Component**: The `calculator_run_interactive` function manages the interactive mode, displaying a menu and reading input values. It validates the input and performs the operation, handling errors appropriately.

3. **Main Entry Point**: The `main` function determines the mode based on command-line arguments. In interactive mode, it calls `calculator_run_interactive`. In non-interactive mode, it parses the command-line arguments, validates them, and performs the operation.

This structure ensures that the calculator app is modular, handles invalid inputs gracefully, and supports both interactive and non-interactive modes.
